# Left out: setting up result (it's appended to but never created, GAP001),
# what to return at the end, and how duplicate triples are avoided (only
# mentioned, so it's an edge-case note). There is no return anywhere, so
# the analyzer has no GAP003 to report for the missing one; the gold just
# has no return node.

step 1
add root: nums.sort()
expect gaps none
or
add root: nums = sorted(nums)
expect gaps none

step 2
add root: for i in range(len(nums)~range-bounds):
    ...
expect gaps GAP002

step 3
fill h1:
    left = i + 1
    right = len(nums) - 1
expect gaps WARN001 WARN001

step 4
add n6: while left < right:
    total = nums[i] + nums[left] + nums[right]
expect gaps WARN001

step 5
add n25:
    if total == 0:
        result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])
        left += 1
        right -= 1
expect gaps GAP001
or
add n25:
    if total == 0:
        result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])
        left = left + 1
        right = right - 1
expect gaps GAP001

# Adding an elif to an existing if: the console has no command for it, so
# the step replaces the if with the same first branch plus the new ones.
step 6
replace n43:
    if total == 0:
        result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])
        left += 1
        right -= 1
    elif total < 0:
        left += 1
    else:
        right -= 1
expect gaps GAP001
or
replace n43:
    if total == 0:
        result.append([nums[i], nums[left], nums[right]])
        left += 1
        right -= 1
    elif total < 0:
        left = left + 1
    else:
        right = right - 1
expect gaps GAP001

step 7
note root edge-case: the same triple can't show up twice in the answer
expect gaps GAP001
